const opts = parseArgs(process.argv.slice(2));

// ── Prime: AI agent instructions ───────────────────────────────────────────
if (opts.prime) {
  console.log(`# spotlight-md — AI Agent Guide

## What this tool does

spotlight-md renders a markdown file as a dark-themed HTML page with
live reload. A human reviewer reads the document in their browser and
highlights passages that need attention. Each highlight becomes a
conversation thread: the human leaves a note, you (the AI) respond with
a comment and edit the markdown, the human replies with more feedback,
and so on until the issue is resolved.

Highlights are stored in a SQLite sidecar database (NOT in the markdown
itself) so your edits to the markdown never conflict with the highlight
annotations. Both the CLI and the web server can read/write
concurrently thanks to WAL mode.

## The review loop

Each highlight is a conversation thread with messages from "human" and
"ai" authors. The loop works like this:

1. You edit the markdown document and save it.
2. The browser auto-reloads. The human reads it and highlights passages.
   They may attach a note to each highlight.
3. You poll for highlights that need attention:
     spotlight-md --needs-attention path/to/document.md
   This lists highlights where the last message is from the human AND
   the highlight is not resolved. These are the ones waiting for you.
4. For each one: edit the markdown to address the issue, then add a
   comment explaining what you changed:
     spotlight-md --add-comment hl-abc123 --comment-text "Rewrote paragraph 3 with a concrete example" path/to/document.md
5. The human sees your comment in the right panel. They can reply with
   more feedback, resolve the highlight, or complete it.
6. Repeat from step 3 until --needs-attention returns empty.

You do NOT need to wait for the human to tell you to check. You can
poll --needs-attention in a loop. If it returns nothing, there is no
work to do. If it returns highlights, address them.

## Commands

### Poll for work (highlights needing your attention)

  spotlight-md --needs-attention path/to/document.md

Returns only highlights where:
  - the last message is from the human (they are waiting for you)
  - the highlight is not resolved or completed

Output format:
  Highlights for document.md (N total, X open, Y resolved, Z completed):

  hl-abc123  §section-id
    "preview of highlighted text..."
    [YOU] First note from the human
    [AI]  Your previous response (if any)
    [YOU] Human's follow-up feedback
    created: 2026-07-26T...

### List all highlights (including resolved and completed)

  spotlight-md --list-highlights path/to/document.md

### Add an AI comment to a highlight (after addressing the issue)

  spotlight-md --add-comment hl-abc123 --comment-text "What you changed and why" path/to/document.md

Use comments to explain what you changed. The human sees them in the
right panel with a blue border (distinct from their own messages which
have an accent border). This closes the feedback loop.

### Resolve a highlight (when you believe the issue is fully addressed)

  spotlight-md --resolve-highlight hl-abc123 path/to/document.md

Resolved highlights are dimmed in the panel and hidden by the default
filter. The human can reopen them if they disagree.

### Complete a highlight (marks it done, preserves history)

  spotlight-md --delete-highlight hl-abc123 path/to/document.md

Despite the flag name, this does NOT delete the highlight. It marks it
completed and preserves the full conversation thread in the database.
Use this only when the human explicitly asks you to clean up.

### Mark all highlights as completed (only when the human asks)

  spotlight-md --clear-highlights path/to/document.md

### Serve a document for the human to review

  spotlight-md --auto --theme dracula path/to/document.md

This starts a local server (default port 7231) and opens the browser.
The human can select text to create highlights (with optional notes),
reply to highlights in the right panel, resolve them with a checkmark,
or complete them. The server watches the file — when you save changes
to the markdown, the browser reloads automatically.

## Highlight data model

Each highlight has:
  - id:           short identifier (e.g. hl-abc123)
  - text:         the exact passage the human highlighted
  - sectionId:    HTML id of the nearest preceding heading
  - sectionTitle: heading text for context
  - resolved:     boolean — you or the human marked it resolved
  - completed:    boolean — marked done (history preserved)
  - messages:     array of {author: "human"|"ai", text, createdAt}
  - createdAt:    ISO timestamp
  - completedAt:  ISO timestamp (if completed)

## Sidecar storage

Highlights are stored in:  <filename>.highlights.db (SQLite, WAL mode)
This file is managed by spotlight-md. Do not edit it by hand. Do not
commit it to version control (add *.highlights.db to .gitignore).

## Workflow rules

- NEVER complete or delete highlights unless the human explicitly asks.
  Highlights remain until the human decides they are done.
- Poll --needs-attention to find work. If empty, there is nothing to do.
- When you address a highlight, ALWAYS add a comment explaining what
  you changed. The human relies on comments to verify your work without
  reading the full diff.
- After editing the markdown, save the file. The browser auto-reloads.
- Pay attention to the full conversation thread — the human may have
  replied to your earlier comment with new feedback.
- The highlight text is the exact passage from the document at the time
  the human selected it. Use it to locate the relevant section. If you
  edit that passage, the highlight may no longer render in the browser
  (the text changed) — that is expected and signals the issue was
  addressed.
- You can resolve a highlight when you believe it is fully addressed.
  The human can reopen it if they disagree.`);
  process.exit(0);
}

